From 941c7edcd888f4291f2c177e30e23bdb60bd3a44 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 18:19:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] cuda/hip: a dedicated f32 GEMM for the skinny prefill matmuls
 (+7.3% prefill)

qwen35moe issues three plain f32 matmuls per layer that the vendor BLAS handles
badly at prefill: the MoE router [2048 -> 256] on all 40 layers, and ssm_alpha
and ssm_beta [2048 -> 32] on the 30 linear-attention layers. rocBLAS picks a
64x64x16 macro tile for all of them, and with 32 or 256 output rows that is
**8 and 32 workgroups on a 64 CU part**. Measured at pp512: 60 launches of
119.4 us and 40 of 124.3 us, 12.1 ms of a 106.8 ms pass - 11.4%, against a
memory floor of roughly 9 us and 27 us respectively.

The problem is parallelism, not arithmetic, and the diagnostic is the dispatch
dimensions rather than the kernel time: divide the grid by the workgroup size
before assuming a vendor kernel is doing something clever.

mul_mat_f32_skinny_cuda gives one wave an NT x MT tile of the output and splits
k across its 32 lanes. The grid is (N/NT) x (M/(MT*nwarps)), so the alpha/beta
shape launches 256 waves instead of 16. Each lane accumulates a k-strided
partial and one warp reduction per output element finishes it - NT*MT reductions
against K/32 iterations of NT*MT fmas each, so the reduction amortises away.
Waves inside a block share n0 and differ in m0, which keeps the src0 rows a
block touches resident in that CU's L1 while the src1 rows stream.

Per kernel: alpha/beta **119.4 -> 23.8 us (5.0x)**, router **126.1 -> 57.0 us
(2.2x)**; the group goes 12.1 -> 3.7 ms per pass. NT/MT were swept
(4x8, 8x4, 8x8, 8x16, 16x8) and are indistinguishable at the wall - 8x8 ships.

mmf, llama.cpp's own tiled f32/f16 matmul, cannot serve these: on RDNA4 its
WMMA path is compiled out for T=float, and its non-ids path does not tile
columns at all. It never fires anywhere in this model, at either shape.

Measured (runner box, HIP_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0, whole-process interleaved A/B
against a control binary built from the parent commit - LD_LIBRARY_PATH set to
the snapshot and verified with ldd - rotated order, 5 rounds):

  arm                       pp512 med   vs control   tg128 med   vs control
  control (0043 binary)       4625.45      -          148.60       -
  MAX_ROWS=0 (off)            4643.48    0.9967       148.42     1.0003
  MAX_ROWS=64 (alpha/beta)    4822.83    1.0392       148.60     1.0003
  MAX_ROWS=256 (shipped)      4957.11    1.0727       148.48     1.0002

pp arms disjoint 5/5 (min 4941.07 above max control 4697.00); tg arms overlap
the control everywhere. pp4096 confirms it scales: 4118.35 -> 4360.01 (+5.87%).

- gate ppl -c 512 --chunks 8, 3 loads: median **3.9309 vs stock 3.9314**,
  -0.013%. The 0043 stack alone reads -0.262%, so this round's ULP draw moved
  the reading back toward stock rather than further from it - more evidence
  that the shift is a random walk through the router's top-8, not damage
- decode-path ppl -b 512 -ub 1 --chunks 8: 3.9338 both arms - M = 1 is below
  the column threshold, so the kernel is gated off the decode shape
- ragged ubatches, which take the bounds-checked instantiation: -b 500 -ub 100
  4.7366 -> 4.7300, -ub 48 4.7679 -> 4.7719
- test-backend-ops MUL_MAT 1186/1186, seven runs, four of them with the row and
  column gates opened wide so every eligible shape in the suite takes this
  kernel. One earlier run reported a ROCm backend failure and never reproduced;
  see the README
- firing proven by census diff: 200 Cijk_..._S_B_Bias_..._MT64x64x16 launches
  become 200 mul_mat_f32_skinny_cuda
- long context neutral: tg128 @ d16384 136.61 -> 136.55, @ d32768 126.79 -> 126.80
- llama-server greedy 5/6 byte-identical against the off arm; only the long
  prompt differs, which is the only one of the six with enough tokens to reach
  the column threshold
- GGML_F32_SKINNY_MAX_ROWS=0 disables it, 64 restricts it to alpha/beta;
  GGML_F32_SKINNY_MIN_COLS and GGML_F32_SKINNY_TILE re-sweep the gate and tile

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
